          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">Frank's PIA</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">$3,000 / month</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="background: #f6f8fa;">
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">Mary's Personal PIA</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">$800 / month</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">Mary's Claiming Age</td>
            <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; color: #0D2137;">67 (Full Retirement Age)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
        <strong>Result:</strong> Mary’s max spousal entitlement is 50% of $3,000 = <span style="color: #E8761A; font-weight: 700;">$1,500/month</span>. SSA pays her $800 own benefit plus a $700 spousal top-up, bringing her total check to $1,500/month.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
        <strong>Action Taken:</strong> Frank filed for his benefit at age 67 ($3,000/mo), allowing Mary to claim her full $1,500/mo spousal check. Combined household income: <strong>$4,500/month</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario 2 -->
    <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 0.75rem; padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
